When float avoidance moves an in-flow block formatting context root
down from where its collapsed top margin put it, Ladybird takes that
margin back out of the top margin group its parent still holds open,
and places the box past the floats from where the group leaves it.
Chrome 158 agrees with Ladybird on all 12 cases in the test (a flex and
a flow-root box with a -76px margin, an ordinary block, a positive
margin, a box that fits beside the float, and an empty sibling before
it, each with an auto and a definite height). Firefox 151 and WebKit
26.5 agree on the flex, flow-root, ordinary-block and positive-margin
cases; Firefox also moves a box that fits beside the float, and both
place the case with an empty sibling before the box differently.

All three treat a box that avoids floats and gets pushed down like a box
with clearance, so its margin no longer collapses with the margins
before it. Blink does that in BlockLayoutAlgorithm::
HandleNewFormattingContext(), which lays out the box again with its
margin kept apart once it doesn’t fit beside the floats where the
collapsed margin put it. WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::
computedClearDeltaForChild() gives such a box a clearance delta, which
clearFloatsIfNeeded() then applies. And Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::
ReflowBlockFrame() takes a block that can’t intersect floats as one
that may need clearance, and reflows its parent with the clearance it
discovers.
